# Patient files — radiographs, photos, signed consent forms. Real medical data.
# This is the single most important line in the file: without it the build
# context, and every layer built from it, would carry the practice's records.
storage/

# Secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.production.example

# Build output and local caches — regenerated inside the image
.next/
out/
build/
dist/
node_modules/
src/generated/
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# VCS and tooling
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github/
.claude/
.vscode/
.idea/

# Docs, tests and local-only compose — not needed to build the app.
#
# `tests/` is here for a reason beyond tidiness: patient files are read by a
# path computed at runtime (src/lib/files.ts), so Next's tracer gives up on
# working out what the server needs and copies the project root into the
# standalone output. Anything left in the build context therefore ships inside
# the image. Nothing here runs in production — `npm test` needs tsx, which the
# runtime deliberately does not carry.
README.md
docs/
tests/
docker-compose.yml
docker-compose.prod.yml

# Misc
.DS_Store
*.pem
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.vercel
